APPROACH
At first I did not really understand what the weird characters in the enc file were. The output looked like some kind of unicode or encoded text.
I first thought about using some common decoders and checking if it was something like base64. But it did not look like normal encoded data.
Then I checked the source code that was given with the challenge.
''.join([chr((ord(flag[i]) << 8) + ord(flag[i + 1])) for i in range(0, len(flag), 2)])
At first I did not understand what the << 8 was doing. After looking into it I understood that it was shifting the first character by 8 bits and then adding the next character.
So it looked like two characters from the flag were being combined into one character.
The hint said to look for decoders so I realised that instead of trying to decode it like normal encryption I should probably reverse what the given code was doing.

SOLUTION
The encoded text was 慣慤敭祻ㄶ形楴獟楮獴㌴摟潦弸強㤰扡㌷敽
The code takes two characters at a time and does (ord(flag[i]) << 8) + ord(flag[i + 1])
So to reverse it I took the unicode value of each encoded character using ord().
The first character can be recovered using a right shift n >> 8 and the second character can be recovered using n & 255
I used this Python code
enc = "慣慤敭祻ㄶ形楴獟楮獴㌴摟潦弸強㤰扡㌷敽"
flag = ""
for x in enc:
    n = ord(x)
    flag += chr(n >> 8)
    flag += chr(n & 255)
print(flag)
At first I thought I needed some special unicode decoder because of the strange characters. But after looking at the source code I understood that the characters were created by combining two normal characters.
